  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered showers tapering off tonight. Mainly\ndry conditions Wednesday with scattered showers and storms \narriving overnight into Thursday. \n\nExpect scattered showers for the remainder of the afternoon, \ndecreasing in coverage this evening as higher pressure builds in \nfrom the west. Can't rule out a few isolated showers overnight, \notherwise most locations remain dry. \n\nHigh pressure remains in place Wednesday supporting mainly dry \nconditions with temperatures in the upper 60s and low 70s. A surface \nlow pressure system tracks across Canada bringing a series of fronts \nacross the region later Wednesday through Thursday. A warm front \npushes into southern New England Wednesday night accompanied by \nadvection of a plume of above normal moisture. Precipitable water \nvalues rise to 1-1.8\" overnight. Lift provided by the jet positioned \nto the west and warm front will support the return of showers and \nperhaps some embedded storms. There is uncertainty regarding the \nfiner details such as timing and thunderstorm probabilities due\nto spread across model guidance. Showers/storms more likely \narrive in west and central portions of southern New England 11 \nPM to 2 AM arriving in the I-95 corridor 09-12Z. There are \nindications of marginal elevated instability that accompanies \nthe plume of richer moisture. This may support a few embedded \nthunderstorms. This has been reflected across the simulated \nreflectivity in the high-res guidance, some more aggressive than\nothers. This has led to lower confidence on how widespread \nembedded storms/heavy downpours will be. Steering flow appears \nstrong enough to support quicker storm motion which would limit \nthe risk for flash flooding. If there are embedded storms and \ndownpours, can't rule out the potential for poor drainage/urban \nflooding in the more vulnerable spots.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Still unsettled Thursday with showers and thunderstorms,\nwith a low chance that some storms could become strong. \n\nCarrying over from Wednesday night is a continued risk for showers \nand thunderstorms into Thursday, as a stronger shortwave impulse \nmoves through. The main question mark is on if storms could become \nstrong in this timeframe. Although the airmass is plenty humid and \nmid and upper level wind fields are seasonably strong, considerable \ncloud cover could temper how much destabilization occurs. GFS-\nbased CAPE profiles show about 800-1600 J/kg of most-unstable \nCAPE with moist-adiabatic mid-level lapse rates also tempering \ninstability. It's still debatable if storms could become strong \nbut the chances seem low with more tempered instability favored.\nStill, Thursday looks to be a washout with good chances for \nrains during the daytime hours, with more substantial clearing \ntaking place Thursday evening.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Mix of clouds and sun with diurnally driven spot showers\nFriday into the early part of Labor Day Weekend, but drier \nweather most of the time. Dry and seasonable Sunday and on the \nMonday holiday, with onshore breezes.\n\nStill under cyclonic flow aloft for Friday into the early part of \nLabor Day Weekend. This will keep weather pattern somewhat unsettled \nwith a mix of clouds and sun. Spot showers are possible Friday \nand Saturday afternoon but drier weather prevails most of the \ntime. much of the time. Broad high pressure then works in from \nthe north Sunday into Labor Day, leading to onshore breezes, but\nwith partly sunny skies and dry conditions.",
